{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791047336,"nonce":"T9JY9vC8QPUGzqdwvp3TUElMjiQ=","message":"Async functions as core tasks: d80ac1f76acfd65e1a618cbc65b324af0f38eacd (zyntax b4e746dde344480984ff866b58d7debb579d1677, HostTask, zyntax git-bug ff5cd1d3f875bb19c8a07a1fd463fb4574fec0f5c7c6a61619ef2dddbc9a8744). A call of a Zyntax async function returns a caribou.Future of its declared result, and the interface types it Future(T). The state machine runs on a fiber task of the caller's world: each HostTask step advances it without waiting, and the task parks until the Zyntax timer it waits on is due. It is a fiber because a step's Zyntax code may call world functions that park. The future settles with the result read by the declared type, or rejects with the raised error. Grammar languages get sleep -\u003e __zyntax_async_set_timeout as Zyntax's hosts give it, and extern defs are no longer published. Test: caribou-interop/tests/zyntax_async.rs (Wren; overlapping waits, f64 result, nested await). Limits: a 0/false/null result never settles (zyntax ab01238, now priority:high); ZynML has no async methods, so Haxe reaches none; awaiting an extern-declared bridge crashes in Zyntax (zyntax d67dc57). Remaining in this issue: per-fiber handlers and effects through the bridge (with the Haxe-driver gate), the core-heap memory strategy, TypeMeta on TypeDesc, and zrtl on caribou_abi.","files":null}]}